        "input": "5\n1\n0\n2\n00\n10\n3\n010\n001\n100\n6\n001001\n101000\n000000\n111011\n111001\n011000\n7\n0101011\n0001100\n1100011\n0010100\n1010011\n0101000\n0101010",
        "output": "1 \n2 1 \n-1\n-1\n5 3 1 7 6 2 4",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nExplanation of the first testcase: There is only one participant and the only possible ranking is good.\nExplanation of the second testcase: There are two participants and the participant 222 won against the participant 111. Thus the ranking 2,12, 12,1 (that is, player 222 wins the tournament) is good.\nExplanation of the third testcase: One may check that there is not a good ranking. Let us explain why some possible rankings are not good:\n\nThe ranking P1=1,P2=2,P3=3P_1=1, P_2=2, P_3=3P1​=1,P2​=2,P3​=3 is not good because for i=1i=1i=1, j=3j=3j=3 (notice that 1≤i<j≤min⁡(N,i+⌈N2⌉)1\\le i \\lt j \\le \\min(N, i+\\lceil \\frac N2\\rceil)1≤i<j≤min(N,i+⌈2N​⌉) since ⌈N2⌉=2\\lceil \\frac N2\\rceil=2⌈2N​⌉=2) the result of the game between Pi=1P_i=1Pi​=1 and Pj=3P_j=3Pj​=3 is not consistent with their ranking positions (player 111 lost against player 333 but 111 has a better ranking position).\nThe ranking P1=3,P2=2,P3=1P_1=3, P_2=2, P_3=1P1​=3,P2​=2,P3​=1 is not good because for i=1i=1i=1, j=2j=2j=2 (notice that 1≤i<j≤min⁡(N,i+⌈N2⌉)1\\le i \\lt j \\le \\min(N, i+\\lceil \\frac N2\\rceil)1≤i<j≤min(N,i+⌈2N​⌉) since ⌈N2⌉=2\\lceil \\frac N2\\rceil=2⌈2N​⌉=2) the result of the game between Pi=3P_i=3Pi​=3 and Pj=2P_j=2Pj​=2 is not consistent with their ranking positions."
      }
